.PP
The empty input yields the initial value (bit\-reflected for reflected
variants) XOR the final XOR value.
.SH OUTPUT FORMATS
.TP
.B text
For one input, the line
.B Processed N bytes.
followed by one line per variant: the name padded to 25 columns, a colon, a
space and four hexadecimal digits.
With several inputs each block is headed by
.B ==> FILE <==
and separated from the next by an empty line.
.TP
.B csv
Header line
.BR file,bytes,algorithm,crc ,
then one row per file and variant.
Fields containing commas, quotes or newlines are quoted.
.TP
.B tsv
The same columns, separated by tabs; nothing is quoted.
.TP
.B json
An array with one object per file, for example:
.RS
.PP
.EX
[
  {"file": "a.bin", "bytes": 9, "crc": {"CRC\-16/ARC": "BB3D"}}
]
.EE
.RE
.PP
Standard input is named
.B \-
in the structured formats.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
At least one input could not be read; the remaining inputs are still
processed.
Also returned by
.B \-\-self\-test
on a mismatch and on output errors.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error (unknown option or algorithm, missing or invalid argument).
.PP
Errors are written to standard error as
.BR "crc16: FILE: message" .
.SH EXAMPLES
All variants of a file:
.PP
.RS
.EX
$ crc16 firmware.bin
.EE
.RE
.PP
Only the Modbus and X\-25 CRCs, without the byte count, in lowercase:
.PP
.RS
.EX
$ crc16 \-q \-x \-a modbus,x\-25 firmware.bin
.EE
.RE
.PP
CSV for several files, single\-threaded:
.PP
.RS
.EX
$ crc16 \-j 1 \-f csv \-a arc a.bin b.bin
.EE
.RE
.PP
From a pipe:
.PP
.RS
.EX
$ zcat image.gz | crc16 \-a xmodem
.EE
.RE
.SH STANDARDS
The program is written in portable ISO C.
The minimum standard is
.B C11
(for
.IR <threads.h> ).
Built with
.B \-DCRC16_NO_THREADS
or on a system without
.IR <threads.h> ,
it needs only
.B C99
and runs single\-threaded.
Reading regular files through
.BR mmap (2)
and counting CPUs use POSIX interfaces when available, with ISO fallbacks.
The x86\-64 carry\-less multiply path is selected at run time from the CPU
features and is optional; build with
.B make SIMD=0
for the portable code only.
.SH NOTES
.SS How it is fast
Variants that share the polynomial and reflection differ only in the initial
value and final XOR, and a CRC register is linear over GF(2).
The 31 variants therefore collapse into 15 groups, each needing one pass over
the data plus a cheap per\-variant fix\-up at the end.
Each pass uses slicing\-by\-8 tables or, on x86\-64 CPUs with PCLMULQDQ,
carry\-less multiply folding.
Large inputs are cut into pieces that worker threads process independently
and merge with the CRC combine identity.
.SS Omitted variants
The following are deliberately not implemented:
Fletcher (not a CRC), OpenPGP and CAN\-FD (not CRC\-16), SICK (a different
algorithm), and EFI, G\-3, EVEREST and ADCCP (no validated specification).
.SH BUGS
A file that is truncated by another process while it is being mapped can
terminate the program with
.BR SIGBUS ;
use
.B \-\-no\-mmap
to avoid this.
.PP
File names are written byte for byte: TSV output does not escape tabs or
newlines in them, and JSON output is valid only for UTF\-8 names.
.PP
.B \-j 0
counts the online CPUs and ignores CPU affinity masks and container quotas.
.PP
The names are those of the RevEng catalogue and its aliases; some protocols
add framing (for example Bluetooth or DF\-1) that is outside the CRC itself.
.SH AUTHORS
Written by Daniel Dias Rodrigues
.RI < danieldiasr@gmail.com >.
.PP
Speed techniques follow Mark Adler's slicing tables (as used by zlib and
crcspeed by Matt Stancliff) and Intel's carry\-less multiply folding paper;
the code is original.
